<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin of Tours</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 316–<a href="/years/397">397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Martin, Saint Martin of Tours</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> was a Roman soldier turned monk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Tours from 371 until his death in 397. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, he is remembered for sharing his cloak with a beggar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, and he founded the monasteries of Ligugé and Marmoutier. His shrine at Tours became one of the greatest pilgrimage centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and he was adopted as the patron saint of the Frankish kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours.jpg" alt="Martin of Tours" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simone Martini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_Martini_040.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Martin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaria">Savaria</a> in the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, now Szombathely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. His father was a tribune in the Roman army, who was given veteran status and land at Ticinum, now Pavia, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where Martin grew up. At the age of ten he attended a Christian church against his parents' wishes and became a catechumen. He served in the Roman cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and his contemporary biographer Sulpicius Severus tells how at the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> he cut his military cloak in half to share it with a beggar. That night he dreamed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> wearing the half he had given, and he was baptised at 18.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Martin left military service before 361 and became a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers">Hilary of Poitiers</a>, whose Nicene orthodoxy he shared in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> of the imperial court. In Illyricum he took the Nicene side with so much zeal that he was publicly whipped and made to leave. He established the monastery at Ligugé.</p><p>In 371 he succeeded Litorius as the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. According to one tradition he hid in a barn full of geese to avoid it, but their cackling gave him away. He ordered the destruction of pagan temples, altars and sculptures. In 385 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> ordered the beheading of the ascetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillian">Priscillian</a> and his followers. Martin, though strongly opposed to their teaching, had travelled to the imperial court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to reject Bishop Ithacius's principle of putting heretics to death and the emperor's intrusion into such matters, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a> also did. Maximus granted him a promise to spare Priscillian, then broke it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Martin died at Candes-Saint-Martin in 397. People of Poitou and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> quarrelled over his burial, and residents of Tours carried the body by boat down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> to their city. His shrine became one of the most prominent establishments in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Charlemagne made his adviser Alcuin abbot, and the scriptorium at Tours developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a>. The abbey was ransacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 853 and 903.</p><p>The half of the cloak he kept became a relic of the Merovingian kings, first attested in the royal treasury in 679, and was carried into battle and used for oaths. He is the patron of beggars, tailors, vintners and geese, and of cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaria">Szombathely</a> and Utrecht, and is usually shown on horseback dividing his cloak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martin of Tours” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 4th century it became the see of <b>Saint Martin of Tours</b>, and his shrine made it one of the great pilgrimage centres of Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/state/turones">Turones</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Savaria, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Candes-Saint-Martin, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.23,16.62&amp;year=368" aria-label="Savaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Martin of Tours"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4230 6005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4230 6005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.23,16.62&amp;year=368">What was here in 368 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
